\description{
Reports the proportion of each group whose outcome reaches a meaningful
threshold (a minimal clinically important difference, a remission cut,
a mastery criterion), a clinical and behavioral endpoint that a mean
difference does not show. For each group the function reports the
responder count and proportion with a Wilson confidence interval; with
exactly two groups it
adds the risk difference with the Newcombe (1998) score-based hybrid
interval and the number needed to treat with its confidence set; and
across any number of groups it reports the omnibus chi square test of
equal responder proportions. An optional \code{sweep} repeats the
analysis over a grid of thresholds, making explicit how conclusions
depend on where the line is drawn, disclosing the threshold dependence
that any single-threshold claim leaves implicit.
}
\details{
\strong{What is estimated.} For each group, the proportion of its
population whose outcome is at or beyond the threshold, \eqn{\pi_g}.
With two groups, the difference of the two proportions,
\eqn{\Delta = \pi_2 - \pi_1} (second level of \code{group} minus the
first), and the number needed to treat, \eqn{1/|\Delta|}: the number of
people who would have to be moved from the first condition to the
second for one more of them, on average, to be a responder. This is the
number needed to treat of evidence based medicine, a function of two
response proportions at a stated threshold. It is not the quantity
\code{\link{nnt_from_smd}} returns, which is computed from a
standardized mean difference under a normal model, involves no
threshold, and is smaller for a positive effect (see \emph{Which NNT
this is} on that page).

\strong{Assumptions.} The observations are independent, and with two
groups the groups are independent of each other (two arms of a
randomized trial, not two occasions for the same people; for paired
proportions see \code{\link{ci_proportion_paired}}). The threshold is
fixed in advance. Every interval and the test condition on the
threshold as a known constant, so a threshold chosen after the data are
seen, for instance the one at which the difference is largest, makes
the stated confidence level and the \emph{p}-value wrong, and no
adjustment is made for it here. \code{sweep} describes how the result
depends on the threshold; it is not a search, and the rows of a sweep
are not adjusted for one another.

\strong{The interval for each proportion.} With \eqn{r} responders
among \eqn{n}, \eqn{p = r/n}, and \eqn{z} the \eqn{1 - \alpha/2}
quantile of the standard normal distribution, the Wilson score limits
are
\deqn{\frac{p + z^2/(2n) \mp z \sqrt{p(1 - p)/n + z^2/(4n^2)}}{1 + z^2/n},}
the same limits \code{\link{ci_proportion}} returns. They lie inside
[0, 1] for every count, the counts 0 and \eqn{n} included, where the
limit at the boundary is exactly 0 or 1 (and can be computed as a number
within about \eqn{10^{-16}} of it).

\strong{The interval for the difference.} The two-group risk
difference uses Newcombe's method 10: the difference interval is
assembled from the two Wilson limits, which keeps it inside [-1, 1] and
well behaved at boundary counts. With \eqn{(l_1, u_1)} and
\eqn{(l_2, u_2)} the Wilson limits of the first and second group and
\eqn{d = p_2 - p_1}, the limits computed are
\deqn{d - \sqrt{(p_2 - l_2)^2 + (u_1 - p_1)^2} \quad \mbox{and} \quad
      d + \sqrt{(u_2 - p_2)^2 + (p_1 - l_1)^2}.}

\strong{The number needed to treat and its confidence set.} The
\code{nnt} row holds \eqn{1/|d|}, an absolute value; the direction is
the sign of the \code{difference} row. Its limits come from the limits
of the difference, and what they are depends on where zero falls:
\itemize{
  \item Zero outside the interval for the difference: the reciprocals
    of the two limits, in absolute value, are the limits of the number
    needed to treat, the smaller in \code{ci_lower} and the larger in
    \code{ci_upper}.
  \item Zero inside the interval: a difference of zero is a number
    needed to treat of infinity, so the confidence set is every value
    at least as large as the reciprocal of one limit in one direction,
    together with every value at least as large as the reciprocal of
    the other limit in the other direction. These are two intervals that
    meet at infinity (Altman, 1998), which one lower and one upper limit
    cannot hold, so the two limits of the \code{nnt} row are \code{NA}.
}
In both cases the set is recorded in the \code{nnt_confidence_set}
attribute on the signed scale \eqn{1/\Delta}, where a negative value
means that the first group responds more often, and when it is two
intervals a line under the table states it, as
\code{\link{nnt_from_smd}} does. The set has the coverage of the
interval for the difference, because it is the image of that interval
under the reciprocal.

\strong{The omnibus test.} Pearson's chi square test of equal responder
proportions across the groups, without a continuity correction
(\code{\link[stats]{chisq.test}} with \code{correct = FALSE}), on the
number of groups minus 1 degrees of freedom. With two groups it tests
\eqn{\Delta = 0}. It and the Newcombe interval are different
constructions and can disagree near the boundary of significance.

\strong{Edge cases.}
\itemize{
  \item Equal observed proportions: the difference is 0, the \code{nnt}
    row is \code{Inf}, its limits are \code{NA}, and the confidence set
    is two intervals.
  \item No responder, or only responders, in one group: the Wilson
    limits stay inside [0, 1] and every row is reported. With none in
    one group and all in the other the difference is 1 or \eqn{-1} and
    the number needed to treat is 1.
  \item No responder, or only responders, in every group: the
    proportions and their limits are reported, the difference is 0, and
    the chi square statistic is 0/0, so \code{chi_square} and
    \code{p_value} are \code{NaN}.
  \item A group with one observation: its Wilson interval is reported
    (for one responder of one at the 95 percent level, 0.207 to 1), and
    so is everything built from it. The chi square approximation is
    poor with expected counts this small; no warning is given.
  \item Outcomes equal to the threshold count as responders under both
    directions, so the counts under \code{"ge"} and \code{"le"} sum to
    more than the group size when there are such ties.
  \item Missing values in \code{x} or \code{group} are removed before
    anything is counted. Fewer than two groups after that is an error,
    as are fewer than four values in \code{x}.
  \item More than two groups: one row per group and the
    \code{omnibus} row. There is no \code{difference} or \code{nnt}
    row and no \code{nnt_confidence_set} attribute.
}

\strong{A worked example.} In the first example below, 30 of 60
control participants and 35 of 60 treated participants gained at least
10 points. The responder proportions are 0.500 (95 percent Wilson
interval 0.377 to 0.623) and 0.583 (0.457 to 0.699). The difference is
0.083, with the Newcombe interval \eqn{-0.093} to 0.252. The number
needed to treat is 1/0.083 = 12. Because the interval for the
difference includes zero, the \code{nnt} row has no limits; the
confidence set is the two intervals whose finite ends are the
reciprocals of the limits of the difference,
\eqn{1/(-0.093) = -10.8} and \eqn{1/0.252 = 3.97}: a number needed to
treat of 3.97 or more in favor of treatment, or of 10.8 or more in
favor of control. The chi square statistic is 0.839 on 1 degree of
freedom, \emph{p} = 0.360. The second example repeats the analysis at
thresholds of 5 and 15: at 5 the difference is 0.183 (0.026 to 0.330),
its interval excludes zero, and the number needed to treat is 5.45
with limits 3.03 and 37.98; at 15 the difference is 0.067 and its
interval includes zero again. One trial thus gives three different
answers at three thresholds, which is the reason to fix the threshold
first and to show the sweep.

\strong{What the threshold costs.} Dichotomizing an outcome
discards information. For two variables that are bivariate normal,
MacCallum, Zhang, Preacher, and Rucker (2002, pp. 24 to 26) show that
splitting one of them at its mean multiplies their correlation by 0.798
and their squared correlation by 0.637, which for a test of zero
correlation is about the same as discarding 36 percent of the sample,
and that the loss grows as the split moves away from the mean. A
responder threshold is such a split of the outcome. A responder
analysis therefore complements, and never replaces, the analysis of
the continuous outcome (Maxwell, Delaney, & Kelley, 2027).
}
\examples{
# A two-arm trial: change scores, response defined as a gain of 10+.
# The interval for the difference includes zero, so the NNT row has no
# limits and the line under the table gives the two intervals.
set.seed(113)
change <- c(rnorm(60, 8, 9), rnorm(60, 13, 9))
arm    <- rep(c("control", "treatment"), each = 60)
res <- responder_analysis(change, arm, threshold = 10)
res
attr(res, "nnt_confidence_set")

# How threshold-dependent is that conclusion?
responder_analysis(change, arm, threshold = 10, sweep = c(5, 15))

}
